HUB.add({
  id: 'security', title: 'Security', short: 'Security',
  blurb: 'Added: XSS, tokens, CORS, secrets, dependencies. Senior interviews almost always ask.',
  items: [
{ q: `How does React protect against XSS, and where can it still happen?`,
  k: `React wipes dirty words off letters before reading them aloud, unless you tell it "trust this".`,
  a: `React escapes strings rendered in JSX, so injected text shows as text. Risks remain with <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> (sanitize with DOMPurify), <code>javascript:</code> URLs in <code>href</code> (validate protocols), user-controlled <code>style</code>/attributes spread from untrusted objects, server-rendered JSON embedded unsafely, and third-party scripts. Add a Content Security Policy.` },
{ q: `Where should you store auth tokens in a React app?`,
  k: `Keep the wristband in your pocket and the membership card in a locker only staff can open.`,
  a: `Access token in memory (short-lived), refresh token in an <b>httpOnly, Secure, SameSite</b> cookie set by the server; on reload call a refresh endpoint. localStorage is readable by any injected script. Or use cookie-only sessions (BFF pattern) with CSRF protection. Preventing XSS comes first either way.` },
{ q: `What is CORS and who fixes a CORS error?`,
  k: `The shop's guard only accepts letters from houses on its list.`,
  a: `The browser blocks cross-origin requests unless the server returns <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> (and related headers; preflight for non-simple requests; credentials need an explicit origin). Fixed on the <b>server</b>; in development use a dev-server proxy. It's not a security feature for the server, it protects users' browsers.` },
{ q: `Is it safe to put API keys in front-end environment variables?`,
  k: `Writing on the outside of the envelope: everyone can read it.`,
  a: `No. <code>VITE_*</code>, <code>NEXT_PUBLIC_*</code>, <code>REACT_APP_*</code> values are bundled into JavaScript anyone can read. Only public identifiers belong there. Keep secrets on a server or serverless function and proxy calls.` },
{ q: `What is CSRF and how do you prevent it?`,
  k: `A bad website tricks your browser into sending a letter with your wristband.`,
  a: `Cross-site request forgery abuses automatically sent cookies. Prevent with <code>SameSite=Lax/Strict</code> cookies, CSRF tokens (double-submit or synchronizer), checking <code>Origin</code>, and not using GET for mutations. Bearer tokens in headers aren't sent automatically, so they're not CSRF-prone (but are XSS-prone).` },
{ q: `How do you keep dependencies secure?`,
  k: `Check every toy someone gives you for broken parts.`,
  a: `Lockfiles, <code>npm audit</code>/Dependabot/Renovate, minimal dependencies, pin and review updates, avoid freshly published versions (supply-chain attacks), Subresource Integrity for CDN scripts, and keep React/framework patched (React Server Components had critical RCE fixes in late 2025).` },
{ q: `Does client-side route protection secure an app?`,
  k: `A sign on the door helps, but the real lock is at the shop.`,
  a: `No. Route guards are UX only; anyone can change JS in the browser. Every API endpoint must check authentication and authorization on the server. Hide UI by role for usability, enforce on the server for security.` },
{ q: `What is a Content Security Policy?`,
  k: `A house rule list saying which helpers are allowed in.`,
  a: `An HTTP header restricting which scripts, styles, images and connections the page may load (<code>script-src 'self'</code>, nonces/hashes for inline scripts, <code>connect-src</code> for APIs). It limits the damage of XSS. Frameworks support nonces for inline scripts.` },
  ],
});

HUB.add({
  id: 'architecture', title: 'Architecture and system design', short: 'Architecture',
  blurb: 'Added: how seniors structure React apps, choose state tools, and design features.',
  items: [
{ q: `How do you structure a large React application?`,
  k: `Each room has its own shelf; shared things live in one known place.`,
  a: `Feature-based folders (<code>features/cart</code> with its components, hooks, service, queries, tests), a shared <code>ui</code>/design-system layer, a <code>lib</code> for API client, query client and utilities, routes as composition points. Clear boundaries: features import from shared, not from each other's internals. Enforce with ESLint import rules; monorepo (Turborepo/Nx) for multiple apps.` },
{ q: `How do you decide where state should live?`,
  k: `Ask: who owns it, and who needs it?`,
  a: `Server data → query cache. URL-shareable (filters, tab, page) → URL. Form inputs → form library or local state. UI state used by one component → local. Shared by a subtree → lift up or context. App-wide frequently updated client state → Zustand/Redux. Derived values → compute, don't store.` },
{ q: `How would you design a product listing page with filters, pagination and a cart?`,
  k: `A shop window: filters at the side, pages of toys, and a basket that remembers.`,
  a: `Filters/sort/page in the URL; list query keyed by those params with <code>keepPreviousData</code> and 1–5 min staleTime; prefetch next page and details on hover; seed detail from list with <code>initialData</code>; virtualize long lists; skeletons; cart as short-staleTime server state with optimistic add-to-cart, but never optimistic checkout; invalidate cart and stock after orders; SSR/SSG for SEO pages.` },
{ q: `What are micro-frontends and when are they worth it?`,
  k: `Several teams each build their own room, and the rooms join into one house.`,
  a: `Splitting a front end into independently built and deployed apps (Module Federation, import maps, iframes, route-level composition). Worth it for many autonomous teams with separate release cycles. Costs: duplicated dependencies, shared state and design consistency problems, harder performance. A modular monorepo is often enough.` },
{ q: `How do you build a design system in React?`,
  k: `One box of official house bricks everyone uses.`,
  a: `Design tokens (color, type, spacing) as CSS variables, accessible primitives (often built on Radix/React Aria), consistent component APIs (variants, sizes, polymorphic <code>as</code>, ref forwarding), Storybook docs, visual and a11y tests, semver releases with changelogs, and theming via tokens.` },
{ q: `How do you approach accessibility in React apps?`,
  k: `Make the house usable for everyone, including people who can't see or use a mouse.`,
  a: `Semantic HTML first (button, label, nav, headings), keyboard support and visible focus, accessible names, ARIA only when needed, focus management for dialogs and route changes, color contrast, reduced motion, form errors announced, testing with axe, screen readers and keyboard-only. Use accessible primitives for complex widgets.` },
{ q: `How do you handle internationalization in React?`,
  k: `The same house with signs in many languages.`,
  a: `Use a library (react-i18next, FormatJS/react-intl, next-intl): message catalogs with ICU plurals, <code>Intl</code> for numbers, dates and currencies, lazy-loaded locales, locale in the URL, RTL support with logical CSS properties, and no string concatenation of translated fragments.` },
{ q: `How do you monitor a React app in production?`,
  k: `Little sensors that tell you when something breaks or gets slow.`,
  a: `Error tracking (Sentry) with source maps and error boundaries, Real User Monitoring of Web Vitals (LCP, INP, CLS) via <code>web-vitals</code>, logging of failed API calls, feature flags for safe rollout, and alerts. Use React's <code>onCaughtError</code>/<code>onUncaughtError</code> root options to report.` },
{ q: `How do you approach a frontend system design interview?`,
  k: `First ask what's needed, then draw the big boxes, then zoom into the tricky parts.`,
  a: `1) Clarify requirements (functional, non-functional: scale, devices, offline, SEO, a11y, i18n). 2) High-level architecture (rendering strategy, routing, components). 3) Data model and API (REST/GraphQL, pagination, real-time). 4) State and caching. 5) Performance (bundles, images, virtualization). 6) Accessibility, security, error handling, observability. 7) Trade-offs and what you'd do next. Narrate decisions.` },
  ],
});
